# knag — non-secret defaults. Copy to .env (this file is committed; .env is not).
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Secrets go in two places, never here:
#   - Cloudflare Worker secrets, via `wrangler secret put`:
#       KNAG_PASSPHRASE      PWA login          (different per environment)
#       KNAG_BEARER_TOKEN    agent / MCP        (different per environment)
#   - .env.local (gitignored), for credentials that stay on this machine:
#       CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN  the DEV account token. Never the prod one — prod
#                             lives only in the GitHub Environment (ADR-002).
#       GH_TOKEN              github identity for ghw

# 🔴 Hosts that `make health` and `make verify` check — one per environment.
#
# A single `HOST` was set here for both, which silently overrode the Makefile's
# per-environment default: `make health ENV=dev` pointed at the prod domain, failed
# to resolve, and had therefore never once verified a dev deployment.
#
# Dev is a *.workers.dev hostname derived from the Cloudflare account; read yours
# with `pnpm exec wrangler whoami`. Prod is whatever custom domain you attach.
#
# 🔴 Placeholders, and they stay placeholders. This file is committed and the repo is
# public — a real dev host here publishes a live login form that the WAF rate-limit
# rule does not cover, because that rule can only be attached to a custom domain
# (spec §4.2). Put the real values in `.env`, which is gitignored.
DEV_HOST=knag-dev.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev
PROD_HOST=knag.example.com

# 🔴 Which Cloudflare account each environment is allowed to touch.
#
# `make deploy` and `make migrate` refuse to run unless the ACTIVE credential
# resolves to the id below. This is not belt-and-braces — wrangler also honours a
# machine-global `wrangler login` stored outside this repo, which no project
# setting overrides. Without the assertion, a stale login silently decides where
# your deploy lands. See scripts/preflight.sh.
#
# Read an id with: pnpm exec wrangler whoami
#
# 🔴 Placeholders. An account id is not a credential, but it identifies a real
# Cloudflare account and this file is committed — it belongs in `.env`, not here.
CF_ACCOUNT_ID_DEV=
CF_ACCOUNT_ID_PROD=
